  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\n   - The Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem requires finding a step function that minimizes the maximum overlap with its complement. We approach this by extending the current solution using hierarchical resolution and frequency-domain perturbations.\n   - The central idea is to evolve the function across multiple scales and apply spectral filtering to disrupt high-correlation coefficients. We start with the provided initial solution and systematically increase resolution through interpolation, then apply low-pass filtering in the frequency domain to smooth transitions and reduce peak correlations.\n   - The bottleneck is optimizing across multiple resolutions while maintaining numerical feasibility. Key limitations include potential local optima and the quadratic cost of correlation operations, but the frequency-filtered representation reduces sharp transitions that drive high correlation peaks.\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\n   - Variables: Represent h(x) as a real-valued step function on [0,2]\n   - Optimization variables: Piecewise constant values at grid points with \u2211h_i * dx = 1\n   - Objective: C5 = max_lag \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx\n   - Exact projection: Use gradient-based projection enforcing 0\u2264h_i\u22641 and \u222bh=1\n   - Discretization: n-point grid (dx=2/n), correlation computed via mode='full'\n   - Relaxation: Constrain function to be bandlimited in frequency domain\n   - Solvers: Gurobi/CVXOPT for exact projections; topk_smooth gradient for evolution\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\n   a) Initialize with initial_h_values and validate constraints\n   b) Hierarchical resolution doubling from base level (n=280)\n   - For each level (n, 2*n, 4*n, ...):\n     1. Interpolate parent solution to new grid and enforce \u222bh=1\n     2. Apply random frequency truncation (beyond user-defined cutoff)\n     3. Run FISTA gradient descent on 3 lowest lags\n     4. Maintain population archive of top candidates\n   c) Candidate acceptance:\n     - Track best C5 across all resolutions\n     - Maintain solution diversity via random restarts\n   d) Termination checks:\n     - Time expiration\n     - Cost improvement plateau\n     - Resolution bound (max_level)\n\n4. Search choices and variations:\n   - Frequency cutoff: Adaptive from [1, n//2]\n   - Multilag optimization: Topk selects [3, min(5, \u221an)] critical lags\n   - Restart strategy: Copy current solution if stagnation >20 generations\n   - Hyperparameter schedule:\n     - Learning rate: Exponential decay (initial 0.02)\n     - Lag weighting: Focus on top 25% correlation peaks\n     - Resolution spacing: Powers of 2 (n=280,560,1120,...)\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness:\n   - Dominant costs: O(n\u00b2) correlation dominates, resolution n costs ~4\u00d7 time of lower resolution\n   - Schedule profiling: Insert wall-clock checks during correlation\n   - Fallback recovery: Return smoothed initial solution if unvalidated\n   - Time allocation: 80% to resolution expansion, 20% to optimization refinement\n\n6. Validation and return contract:\n   - Precompute score twice and require absolute stability <5e-6\n   - Input checks for dimension mismatches\n   - Archive storage using dictionary key: (seed, n)\n   - Final output tuple (h_vec, C5_final, n)\n   - Additional health check: \u222b|h_i-0.5|dx < 3.0 (only if n<1000)",
